en, tropical, hot-growing epiphytic orchid species. As an example of monopodial orchid, the new growth emerges from a central tip on top of the upright, stem-like rhizome. The rhizome is covered in alternate, leaf-bearing sheaths. Strap-shaped leaves up to 50cm long angle out from the main stem. As new leaves appear from the tip, old, lower leaves gradually drop off. A characteristic feature is an aerial root system at the base of the plant. New roots emerge from the main rhizome, following the ascending growing trend. This species has been a major element in developing modern hybrids for its characteristic flat, two-tone, long-lasting flowers. The flowers - 8 to 12cm in diameter, are carried on a 30cm long stems. The inflorescences, often produced in pair, flower simultaneously, are usually shorter than leaves, and carry 7 - 10 densely arranged, fragrant flowers. Petals are slightly smaller than sepals, and rounded. Two lower sepals are disticntly dark, with red tesselated pattern. Dorsal sepal and petals are white or pale pink, with dark brown-red spots around the centre of flower. Small lip is three-lobed, with the two side lobes distinctly wider that other species.","image":"","commonName":"Sander's vanda","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":409767,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Vanda</em> <em>sanderiana</em>","isAgm":false,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":158706,"botanicalName":"<em>Eucalyptus</em> <em>alba</em>","entityDescription":"Fast-growing, evergreen tree, about 15m tall, native to the tropical northern Australia, with smooth powder-white bark that becomes pale salmon pink when outer bark layers are shed, aromatic mid to dark green lance-shaped leaves, and 7 creamy-white flowers in clusters from late winter to spring, followed by rounded to cone-shaped woody fruits","image":"","commonName":"white gum","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlants